> I am writing up a methods appendix for a report. The first part of this section discusses risk modeling:
> ```
> \subsection{Risk modeling}
> 
> We assess fluvial and surface (pluvial) flooding impacts by combining three independent modelling sources: (i) river flood impacts from the PESETA IV analysis \citepalias{feyen2020climate} based on the LISFLOOD hydrological model, (ii) national projections from the UK Climate Change Risk Assessment 2017 (CCRA3) for future flood risk \citep{sayers2020third}, and (iii) COACCH flood impacts \citepalias{lincked2019d23}, which use the GLOFRIS and OSDaMage frameworks to quantify riverine flooding effects on labour, productivity, and infrastructure. We convert each source to a set of temperature‑driven damage functions in monetary terms and then combine them using a Bayesian meta-analysis that treats the three as structurally distinct models of UK flood damages.
> 
> The PESETA IV river flood results \citepalias{feyen2020climate} are derived from LISFLOOD \citep{van2010lisflood}, a large-scale hydrological model that simulates river discharge and inundation under climate change scenarios. LISFLOOD is driven by bias-corrected climate projections and uses detailed river network, soil, and land-use data to estimate changes in flood frequency and magnitude. Flood damages are obtained by overlaying inundation depth and extent on exposure maps (assets and population) and applying depth–damage functions to derive direct economic losses. We extract UK-specific time series of annual expected damages under different climate scenarios and express these as functions of global mean temperature change, using the common temperature pathways from FaIR as the forcing variable. This yields a set of reduced-form damage functions linking temperature to UK river flood damages, which are then converted into monetary losses consistent with our UK macroeconomic baseline.
> 
> The CCRA3 analysis \citep{sayers2020third} provides projections of future UK flood risk across multiple perils (coastal, fluvial, and surface). To isolate fluvial and surface flooding, we subtract coastal flooding damages from the reported total flood damages, using the consistent partitioning in the CCRA3 report. These flood damage estimates are reported at the scale of water management regions within each UK country. For each region, we follow our standard damage-function procedure: we construct time series of projected annual damages under specified climate and development scenarios, then relate these to global mean temperature or time, focusing on the period where climate change is the dominant driver of changes in risk. 
> 
> To calibrate uncertainty for the CCRA3-based damage functions, we exploit the discrepancy between estimated and modelled damages for the 2007 UK flood event. Observed total 2007 flood damages were approximately \textsterling 3.2\,billion, while the corresponding CCRA3-based projections for that event imply damages of \textsterling 10.9\,billion. We interpret the observed value as approximately the 5th percentile of the model-predicted distribution for that year. Under a lognormal (or similar) error structure, this mapping determines an implied standard error; we then assume that the same proportional standard error applies to all projected damage values derived from CCRA3. This procedure yields regional damage functions with explicit uncertainty bands, which we aggregate to a UK-level probabilistic damage function for fluvial and surface flooding.
> 
> The COACCH flood results \citepalias{lincked2019d23} are based on GLOFRIS, a global river flood hazard and risk model, coupled with the OSDaMage framework for translating flood events into economic impacts. GLOFRIS provides high-resolution maps of flood depth and frequency under different climate scenarios, which are combined with spatially explicit exposure and vulnerability data to estimate direct flood losses. OSDaMage then propagates these shocks through the economy to capture impacts on labour, productivity, and infrastructure. We extract UK-level annual expected damages (direct plus selected indirect effects) from these simulations and fit temperature-dependent damage functions following the same reduced-form approach as for PESETA and CCRA3, ensuring consistency in units (millions of pounds, in common price and currency terms) and baseline socioeconomic assumptions where possible.
> 
> For each of the three sources, the outcome is a stochastic damage function that maps global mean temperature (or time, conditional on a temperature pathway) to annual UK flood damages in monetary terms. We then combine these three model-specific damage functions using a Bayesian meta-analysis framework. In this framework, each model provides an estimate of expected damages and an associated uncertainty distribution at each temperature level. We treat the true but unknown flood damage function as a latent process and the three models as conditionally independent, noisy measurements of this process. The Bayesian combination yields a posterior distribution for damages at each temperature, with a central estimate (e.g.\ posterior mean or median) and credible intervals that reflect both within-model uncertainty and between-model (structural) differences. 
> 
> Finally, we convert the combined flood damages into percentage of UK GDP by dividing the annual monetary losses by a harmonised no-damage GDP baseline for the same year. Where applicable, we ensure that price levels, exchange rates, and macroeconomic paths are consistent with the treatment in other sectors. The general meta-analytic framework, assumptions about error structures, and implementation details of the Bayesian combination across sectors are documented in \citet{rising2026comprehensive} and are not repeated here.
