> ```latex
> \subsection{Adaptation modeling}
> 
> To represent the effect of proactive flood adaptation on UK flood damages, we estimate a reduced-form, temperature-dependent damage function with and without adaptation, calibrated to the global results in \citet{lehner2025adaptation}. We interpret their global welfare and adaptation-cost outcomes as noisy observations of how aggregate flood damages respond to (i) changes in global mean surface temperature (GMST) and (ii) sustained adaptation expenditure, and embed this relationship in a simple parametric model suitable for use within our UK macro-damage framework.
> 
> \subsubsection{Data and mapping to temperature}
> 
> We begin from tabulated results in \citet{lehner2025adaptation}, which report global outcomes under multiple adaptation-financing approaches and warming scenarios. For each scenario--adaptation combination, we extract:
> 
> \begin{itemize}
>   \item the baseline welfare level (no additional flood adaptation),
>   \item the welfare under each adaptation approach (``Balanced Budget'', ``Domestic Bonds'', ``International Bonds''),
>   \item the annualised adaptation investment and associated interest payments.
> \end{itemize}
> 
> For each scenario $s$ and adaptation option $a$, we construct:
> 
> \[
> \text{impact.frac}_{s,a}
>   = \frac{W_{s,a}}{W^{\text{baseline}}_{s}},
>   \quad
> \text{invest}_{s,a}
>   = I_{s,a} + r_{s,a},
> \]
> 
> where $W_{s,a}$ is welfare with adaptation, $W^{\text{baseline}}_{s}$ is welfare in the no-adaptation baseline, $I_{s,a}$ is the direct investment in adaptation, and $r_{s,a}$ is the associated interest cost. We then define, for each scenario $s$, two representative ``approaches'':
> 
> \begin{enumerate}
>   \item \emph{No adaptation} ($a = \text{None}$): $\text{impact.frac}_{s,\text{None}}$ is taken from the ``Impact.Absolute'' case, with $\text{invest}_{s,\text{None}} = 0$.
>   \item \emph{Optimal adaptation} ($a = \text{Opt}$): we select the adaptation option that yields the highest $\text{impact.frac}_{s,a}$ among the available approaches, and set $\text{invest}_{s,\text{Opt}}$ to the corresponding total annual adaptation cost.
> \end{enumerate}
> 
> The \citet{lehner2025adaptation} scenarios are defined for two global warming levels by 2100 ($\Delta T_{2100} \approx 2^\circ\text{C}$ and $4^\circ\text{C}$) and two time horizons ($t=2030, 2050$), yielding four $(\Delta T_{2100}, t)$ combinations for each of the ``No'' and ``Optimal'' adaptation cases. To place these results on a common temperature axis, we link them to ensemble GMST projections from FaIR-based scenarios (the same ensemble used for our sectoral analysis). Specifically, we identify ensemble members that reach approximately $2^\circ\text{C}$ or $4^\circ\text{C}$ of GMST increase by 2100, and compute the corresponding mean GMST in 2030 and 2050, which we denote $\Delta T^{(2)}_{2030}$, $\Delta T^{(2)}_{2050}$, $\Delta T^{(4)}_{2030}$, and $\Delta T^{(4)}_{2050}$. This yields a set of calibration points:
> 
> \[
> \left\{\bigl(\Delta T_{s,t}, D_{s,t}^{\text{NA}}, C_{s,t}^{\text{NA}}\bigr),
>        \bigl(\Delta T_{s,t}, D_{s,t}^{\text{A}}, C_{s,t}^{\text{A}}\bigr)\right\},
> \]
> 
> where:
> \begin{itemize}
>   \item $\Delta T_{s,t}$ is the ensemble-mean GMST anomaly at $(s,t)$,
>   \item $D_{s,t}^{\text{NA}}$ and $D_{s,t}^{\text{A}}$ are the corresponding expected annual direct flood damages under No-Adaptation (NA) and Adaptation (A), expressed in billion pounds per year,
>   \item $C_{s,t}^{\text{NA}}=0$ and $C_{s,t}^{\text{A}}$ is the annual adaptation cost (investment plus interest), in billion pounds per year.
> \end{itemize}
> 
> In addition, we include the near-present climate state (circa 2011) with $\Delta T_{2011}$ taken from the same ensemble and $D_{2011}=0$ as an approximate damage reference point, allowing us to anchor the damage function at the recent historical climate.
> 
> \subsubsection{Parametric damage function with adaptation}
> 
> We model the expected direct flood damages $D(\Delta T)$ (in million \pounds\ per year) as a smooth quadratic function of global mean temperature change $\Delta T$ (in $^\circ\text{C}$), consistent with the structure used for other sectors in this report:
> 
> \[
> D(\Delta T) = 1000 \left(\beta_0 + \beta_1 \Delta T + \beta_2 \Delta T^2\right),
> \]
> 
> where $\beta_0, \beta_1, \beta_2$ are regression coefficients. This functional form is applied to both the No-Adaptation and Adaptation cases; adaptation is represented as a proportional scaling of damages associated with a sustained level of annual adaptation spending.
> 
> Concretely, we assume that a constant annual expenditure on flood adaptation, $C$ (in million \pounds/yr), reduces expected damages multiplicatively by a factor:
> 
> \[
> S(C) = \exp\left(-\eta \,\frac{C}{1000}\right),
> \]
> 
> where $\eta > 0$ is an adaptation effectiveness parameter and $C/1000$ converts damages to a billion-pound scale (matching the calibration data). Under this structure:
> 
> \[
> D^{\text{A}}(\Delta T, C) = D^{\text{NA}}(\Delta T)\, S(C)
> = 1000 \left(\beta_0 + \beta_1 \Delta T + \beta_2 \Delta T^2\right)
>    \exp\left(-\eta \,\frac{C}{1000}\right).
> \]
> 
> We estimate $\eta$ jointly with the damage-function coefficients by fitting the model to the combined set of points $\{(\Delta T_{s,t}, D_{s,t}^{\text{NA}}, C_{s,t}^{\text{NA}})\}$ and $\{(\Delta T_{s,t}, D_{s,t}^{\text{A}}, C_{s,t}^{\text{A}})\}$, augmented by the $2011$ baseline.
> 
> \subsubsection{Bayesian estimation}
> 
> We use a Bayesian regression implemented in Stan to estimate the parameters $(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2,\eta)$ and the residual standard deviation $\sigma$ from the calibration data. Let $n$ index calibration points (across scenarios $s$, time horizons $t$, and adaptation regimes). For each point $n$ we observe:
> 
> \[
> \tilde{\Delta T}_n, \quad \tilde{D}_n, \quad \tilde{C}_n,
> \]
> 
> where $\tilde{\Delta T}_n$ is the GMST anomaly, $\tilde{D}_n$ is the reported expected direct flood damage (in billion \pounds/yr, converted to million \pounds/yr in the regression), and $\tilde{C}_n$ is the corresponding annual adaptation cost. We model:
> 
> \[
> \tilde{D}_n
> = 1000 \left(\beta_0 + \beta_1 \tilde{\Delta T}_n + \beta_2 \tilde{\Delta T}_n^2\right)
>   \exp\left(-\eta \,\frac{\tilde{C}_n}{1000}\right)
>   + \varepsilon_n,
> \quad \varepsilon_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2).
> \]
> 
> We place weakly informative priors on the parameters. In particular, we encode the prior belief that, in the absence of adaptation, quadratic terms dominate the temperature–damage relationship at higher warming. Specifically, we centre the prior for $\beta_2$ such that a purely quadratic form would imply damages of approximately $6.9$ billion \pounds/yr at $\Delta T = 2.2^\circ\text{C}$, i.e.\
> 
> \[
> \beta_2 \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\frac{6.9}{2.2^2}, \frac{6.9}{2.2^2}\right),
> \]
> 
> and use broad priors for $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$. For the adaptation effectiveness parameter, we put a diffuse prior on $\eta$ via a Cauchy scale prior on the corresponding regression coefficient, allowing the data to determine the degree of damage reduction achievable per billion pounds of annual adaptation spending.
> 
> Stan is run with four chains, and we extract the joint posterior samples of $(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2,\eta)$, denoted collectively by $\lambda^{(m)}$, $m=1,\dots,M$. For each posterior draw, we compute the implied damage curves with and without adaptation across a range of temperature increases $0 \le \Delta T \le 4^\circ\text{C}$:
> 
> \[
> D^{\text{NA},(m)}(\Delta T)
> = 1000 \left(\beta_0^{(m)} + \beta_1^{(m)} \Delta T + \beta_2^{(m)} \Delta T^2\right),
> \]
> 
> \[
> D^{\text{A},(m)}(\Delta T)
> = D^{\text{NA},(m)}(\Delta T)
>   \exp\left(-\eta^{(m)} \,\frac{\bar{C}}{1000}\right),
> \]
> 
> where $\bar{C}$ is a representative annual adaptation cost inferred from the \citet{lehner2025adaptation} data (the mean of the observed adaptation expenditures in the optimal/adapted cases). From these samples we construct pointwise posterior means and central credible intervals for both curves.
> 
> \subsubsection{Resulting damage functions and implementation}
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:flood-adapt-model} summarises the fitted damage functions with and without adaptation as a function of GMST change. The solid lines show the posterior mean expected direct flood damages under the No-Adaptation and With-Adaptation cases, and the shaded bands indicate the interquartile range (25th–75th percentiles) of the posterior distribution at each temperature. The calibration points from \citet{lehner2025adaptation} (including the near-present reference) are overlaid.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{channels/flooding/model.pdf}
>   \caption{Bayesian fit of temperature-dependent UK flood damages with and without adaptation. Lines show posterior mean expected direct flood damages (million \pounds/yr) as a function of global mean temperature change since pre-industrial. Shaded bands denote the interquartile range of posterior uncertainty. Points show calibration data derived from \citet{lehner2025adaptation} and the near-present baseline.}
>   \label{fig:flood-adapt-model}
> \end{figure}
> 
> In the integrated damage system, we implement this structure as follows. Let $\widehat{D}^{\text{meta}}(\Delta T)$ denote the meta-analytic UK flood damage function (Section~\ref{sec:risk-modeling}) that aggregates across the three underlying hazard models (PESETA, CCRA3, COACCH). For any given year $t$ and Monte Carlo draw $m$, we first evaluate the no-adaptation damages at the realised global temperature anomaly $\Delta T_t^{(m)}$:
> 
> \[
> \widehat{D}_{t}^{\text{NA},(m)} = \widehat{D}^{\text{meta}}(\Delta T_t^{(m)}).
> \]
> 
> We then apply the adaptation scaling implied by the posterior distribution of $\eta$ for a user-specified annual adaptation budget $C_t$ (in million \pounds/yr):
> 
> \[
> \widehat{D}_{t}^{\text{A},(m)}
> = \widehat{D}_{t}^{\text{NA},(m)} \,
>   \exp\left(-\eta^{(m)} \,\frac{C_t}{1000}\right).
> \]
> 
> The transformation is implemented by the function \texttt{flood.adapt.prop(spend.MGBPyr, la)}, which returns the posterior mean and central credible interval for the proportional damage reduction factor $S(C_t)$, conditional on the posterior draws stored in \texttt{model.RData}. This yields a flexible representation of adaptation: different annual spending profiles $C_t$ can be mapped into time- and scenario-dependent reductions in expected flood damages, fully consistent with the global adaptation evidence in \citet{lehner2025adaptation} and the UK-specific meta-analytic damage function.
