> \subsection{Adaptation modeling}
> 
> We represent proactive flood adaptation by estimating a temperature-dependent damage function with and without adaptation, calibrated to the global welfare and adaptation outcomes reported in \citet{lehner2025adaptation}. Their scenarios provide global changes in welfare and associated annual adaptation expenditures under different warming levels and financing approaches. We interpret these as noisy observations of how expected direct flood damages respond to global mean surface temperature (GMST) and sustained adaptation spending.
> 
> From \citet{lehner2025adaptation}, we construct pairs of cases that correspond to (i) no additional adaptation and (ii) an ``optimal'' adaptation choice that maximises welfare for each warming scenario and horizon (approximately $2^\circ$C and $4^\circ$C in 2100, each evaluated at 2030 and 2050). For each case, we infer an equivalent level of expected direct flood damages (in billion \pounds/yr) and the total annual adaptation costs (investment plus interest). We map these cases to GMST anomalies by matching their warming pathways to an ensemble of FaIR-based temperature projections, and also include a near-present reference point (circa 2011) with near-zero additional damages. This yields a small set of calibration points, each with a GMST anomaly, an associated damage level, and an annual adaptation expenditure.
> 
> Expected direct flood damages $D(\Delta T)$ (in million \pounds/yr) are modeled as a quadratic function of GMST change $\Delta T$ (in $^\circ$C),
> \[
> D(\Delta T) = 1000 \left(\beta_0 + \beta_1 \Delta T + \beta_2 \Delta T^2\right),
> \]
> and adaptation is represented as a multiplicative reduction that depends on annual adaptation expenditure $C$ (in million \pounds/yr),
> \[
> D^{\text{A}}(\Delta T, C)
> = D^{\text{NA}}(\Delta T)\,\exp\!\left(-\eta \,\frac{C}{1000}\right),
> \]
> where $D^{\text{NA}}$ is the no‑adaptation damage function, and $\eta>0$ is an adaptation‑effectiveness parameter. The division by $1000$ aligns $C$ with the billion‑pound scale of the calibration data. In this form, a higher sustained adaptation budget produces an exponentially larger proportional reduction in expected damages.
> 
> We estimate $(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2,\eta)$ and a residual standard deviation using Bayesian regression in Stan, fitting the above relationship to the calibration points (no‑adaptation and adaptation cases plus the near‑present baseline). The likelihood treats reported damages as Gaussian around the parametric form, conditional on GMST and adaptation spending. Priors are weakly informative, with the quadratic term centred to be consistent with a baseline assumption that, in the absence of adaptation, quadratic temperature effects dominate at higher warming levels (e.g.\ implying damages of order $6.9$ billion \pounds/yr at $\Delta T \approx 2.2^\circ$C). The adaptation parameter $\eta$ receives a diffuse prior, allowing the data to govern the inferred damage‑reduction per billion \pounds of annual spending.
> 
> Posterior draws from this model produce a family of damage curves with and without adaptation across a range of GMST changes. Figure~\ref{fig:flood-adapt-model} shows the resulting temperature–damage relationship. Solid lines give posterior mean expected direct flood damages under no adaptation and under a representative level of sustained adaptation, with shaded bands indicating the interquartile range of uncertainty. The calibration points from \citet{lehner2025adaptation} and the near‑present reference are overlaid.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{channels/flooding/model.pdf}
>   \caption{Bayesian fit of temperature-dependent UK flood damages with and without adaptation. Lines show posterior mean expected direct flood damages (million \pounds/yr) as a function of global mean temperature change since pre-industrial. Shaded bands denote the interquartile range of posterior uncertainty. Points show calibration data derived from \citet{lehner2025adaptation} and the near-present baseline.}
>   \label{fig:flood-adapt-model}
> \end{figure}
> 
> Within the integrated system, we apply this structure as a multiplicative adjustment to the combined UK flood damage function described above. For each year $t$ and Monte Carlo draw $m$, we first evaluate the no‑adaptation damages $\widehat{D}^{\text{NA},(m)}_t$ at the realised GMST anomaly. Given an assumed annual adaptation budget $C_t$ (in million \pounds/yr), and a posterior draw of $\eta^{(m)}$, we then obtain adapted damages as
> \[
> \widehat{D}^{\text{A},(m)}_t
> = \widehat{D}^{\text{NA},(m)}_t \,
>   \exp\!\left(-\eta^{(m)} \,\frac{C_t}{1000}\right).
> \]
> This yields a probabilistic, spending‑dependent reduction in expected UK flood damages that is consistent with both the global adaptation evidence and the UK‑specific damage estimates used elsewhere in the analysis.
> ```
