> ```latex
> \subsection{Variability and extreme events}
> 
> In addition to long-run expected annual damages, we represent interannual variability and rare extreme flood events by combining historical event data with the climate-driven projections described above. This yields (i) a probabilistic characterization of single-event damages and their frequency, and (ii) stochastic corrections to the deterministic damage paths to approximate 1-in-200 year outcomes.
> 
> We first compile a catalogue of historical flood damage events from two sources: (i) the UK Flood and Coastal Erosion Risk Management (FCERM)/Environment Agency report (``flooding-evidence.xlsx''), which provides minimum, maximum, and best estimates of event damages in pounds, and (ii) the EM-DAT international disaster database, which records total damages (in thousand US dollars) by event and month. All historical losses are converted to 2024 \pounds\ using consumer price indices and average exchange rates. For the FCERM/EA data, we assign event durations in months based on the reported temporal extent and divide total damages by duration to obtain monthly average damages. For EM-DAT, we aggregate records by start year and month, assume a standard event duration of one month, and again convert to monthly damages. The combined dataset thus consists of a set of independent monthly flood-damage realizations, with associated dates and, where available, uncertainty ranges.
> 
> To characterise the distribution of single-event damages, we treat each observed monthly damage as one draw from an underlying, stationary statistical distribution. We fit several candidate distributions to the combined sample of monthly damages (in 2024 \pounds) using maximum likelihood: log-normal, exponential, Weibull, and a Generalized Extreme Value (GEV) distribution. The Weibull distribution provides a flexible and robust fit across the observed range and is adopted as our baseline representation for stochastic event magnitudes. Separately, we estimate the annual frequency of damaging flood events by counting the number of events per calendar year in the historical catalogue and fitting a Poisson distribution to these annual counts. Together, the Weibull and Poisson fits define a simple compound process for total annual damage from discrete events.
> 
> Expected annual damages (EAD) under future climate change are taken from the pre-computed county-level projections (dataset \texttt{rivercombo-adm3.RData}), which provide Monte Carlo paths of annual flood damages $D_{i,t}^{(m)}$ (in million \pounds) for each county $i$, year $t$, and Monte Carlo draw $m$, under multiple climate scenarios. We focus on two aggregated scenarios: a ``Most Likely'' pathway (an ensemble of runs consistent with a likely global temperature trajectory) and a ``High Risk'' pathway (scenario SSP3-7.0). For each scenario and year, we interpolate damages between the original time slices (2020, 2050, 2090) to obtain annual values from 2025 to 2075. We then summarise, for each scenario and year, the mean and 5th/95th percentile of $D_{i,t}^{(m)}$ across Monte Carlo draws at the county level and at the UK total, defining the EAD paths with associated uncertainty.
> 
> To approximate rare extreme outcomes, we construct 1-in-200 year equivalent damages for selected years under the High Risk scenario by superimposing a stochastic ``residual'' component on top of the climate-driven baseline. For a target central year $t^\ast \in \{2030, 2040, 2050\}$, we consider a 10-year window $[t^\ast, t^\ast+9]$ and, for each Monte Carlo draw $m$, we generate additional annual residual damages as follows. In each year $t$ within the window, we draw a Poisson-distributed number of events with the estimated mean annual rate, and then draw an independent Weibull-distributed damage for each event (in 2024 \pounds). The sum of these event damages in year $t$ is converted to million \pounds\ and added to the baseline UK-wide damages for that year and draw.
> 
> We then identify, for each Monte Carlo draw $m$, the year within the 10-year window that yields the highest total UK damage (baseline plus residual). This ``worst year'' is interpreted as the realisation of a rare, high-impact event in that decade. To allocate the additional residual damages from this worst year spatially across counties, we weight counties in proportion to their baseline damages over a reference period. Specifically, for each draw $m$, we compute non-negative weights $w_i^{(m)}$ based on the county-level damages in 2011–2030 under SSP3-7.0, normalised to sum to one over $i$. The UK-wide residual amount is then distributed across counties according to $w_i^{(m)}$, and added to the corresponding county-level baseline damages in the worst year. This process yields, for each county $i$ and draw $m$, a synthetic realisation of an extreme flood-damage outcome associated with the target decade.
> 
> Finally, for each target year $t^\ast$ and for each county $i$, we summarise the distribution of these worst-year damages across Monte Carlo draws by computing the mean and 5th/95th percentiles. Aggregating over counties gives UK-wide extreme-damage summaries. We interpret these as 1-in-200 year equivalent damages conditioned on the High Risk climate pathway, recognising that they are approximated via a compound-event process calibrated to historical variability.
> 
> All steps are implemented in R. The resulting EAD and extreme-damage estimates at county and UK levels are stored in \texttt{dataset.csv} and visualised in the figures below.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/residuals.pdf}
>   \caption{Historical monthly flood damages and fitted distributions. Segments show per-month damages (in 2024 \pounds) for individual events derived from UK FCERM/Environment Agency reports and EM-DAT, with shaded rectangles indicating reported uncertainty ranges where available. The combined series forms the empirical basis for estimating the distribution of single-event damages and their temporal variability.}
>   \label{fig:residuals}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/dataset-uk.pdf}
>   \caption{Projected UK-wide flood damages under ``Most Likely'' and ``High Risk'' climate scenarios. Solid lines show the mean Expected Annual Damages (EAD) in million \pounds, with shaded bands indicating the 5th–95th percentile range across Monte Carlo draws. Red vertical error bars indicate the 5th–95th percentile range of 1-in-200 year equivalent extreme damages for selected decades under the High Risk scenario, offset slightly in time to distinguish them from the EAD trajectories.}
>   \label{fig:dataset-uk}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/dataset-adm3.pdf}
>   \caption{County-level (ADM3) flood damages under ``Most Likely'' and ``High Risk'' climate scenarios. Each faint line traces the Expected Annual Damages (EAD) for a single county (in million \pounds), illustrating spatial heterogeneity and temporal trends. Red jittered points show the upper end of the 5th–95th percentile range for 1-in-200 year equivalent extreme damages for individual counties in selected decades under the High Risk scenario, highlighting the potential for localized, high-impact events.}
>   \label{fig:dataset-adm3}
> \end{figure}
