> ```latex
> \subsection{Variability and extreme events}
> 
> To represent interannual variability and rare extremes in UK flood damages, we overlay a simple event-based stochastic process on top of the climate-driven damage projections described above.
> 
> We first construct an empirical set of historical flood-damage events from two sources: (i) the UK Flood and Coastal Erosion Risk Management / Environment Agency evidence report, which provides minimum, maximum, and best estimates of event damages in pounds, and (ii) the EM-DAT disaster database, which reports total damages in thousand US dollars. All values are converted to 2024 \pounds\ using consumer prices and exchange rates, and expressed as monthly damages by dividing by event duration (or assuming a one-month duration where this is not reported). The resulting series of per-month damages is treated as a sample of independent event realizations.
> 
> We fit several candidate distributions to these observed monthly damages by maximum likelihood (log-normal, exponential, Weibull, and Generalized Extreme Value). A two-parameter Weibull distribution provides a good compromise between flexibility and parsimony and is adopted as the baseline for event magnitudes. Separately, we estimate the annual number of damaging events as a Poisson process by fitting a Poisson distribution to the historical counts of events per year. Together, the Weibull and Poisson fits define a stochastic process for annual residual flood losses around the smooth damage paths.
> 
> Future EAD at county level $D_{i,t}^{(m)}$ comes from the riverine flood projections, interpolated to annual values between the original time slices and summarised across Monte Carlo draws $m$ for each climate scenario. To approximate 1-in-200 year damages under the High Risk scenario, we focus on three central years (2030, 2040, 2050). For each central year, we consider a 10-year window and, for each Monte Carlo draw, simulate additional annual residual damages within that window by drawing a Poisson number of events and Weibull-distributed event magnitudes (calibrated to historical data). Summing these residuals and adding them to the baseline UK-wide damages for each year in the window, we then identify the year with the highest total damage for that draw. This ``worst year'' is interpreted as one realisation of a rare extreme event in that decade.
> 
> To distribute the additional residual damages spatially within the UK, we allocate the UK-wide residual for each Monte Carlo draw across counties in proportion to their baseline damages in a recent reference period (2011–2030 under SSP3-7.0), using non-negative weights that sum to one over $i$. Adding these weighted residuals to $D_{i,t}^{(m)}$ in the selected worst year yields county-level extreme-damage realisations. We summarise their mean and 5th/95th percentiles by county and in aggregate for the UK, and store the results for downstream analysis.
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:residuals} shows the historical monthly damages used to calibrate the magnitude and frequency distributions of events. Figures~\ref{fig:dataset-uk} and \ref{fig:dataset-adm3} present the resulting EAD paths and associated extreme-damage summaries at UK and county scales.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/residuals.pdf}
>   \caption{Historical monthly UK flood damages in 2024 \pounds\ from Environment Agency / FCERM reports and EM-DAT. Segments represent per-month damages for individual events, with shaded bands indicating reported uncertainty where available.}
>   \label{fig:residuals}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/dataset-uk.pdf}
>   \caption{Projected UK-wide flood damages. Lines show mean Expected Annual Damages (EAD) in million \pounds\ under ``Most Likely'' and ``High Risk'' climate scenarios, with shaded bands giving the 5th–95th percentile range across Monte Carlo draws. Red error bars indicate the 5th–95th percentile range of 1-in-200 year equivalent damages for selected decades under the High Risk scenario.}
>   \label{fig:dataset-uk}
> \end{figure}
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/adapt/flooding/dataset-adm3.pdf}
>   \caption{County-level flood damages. Faint lines show Expected Annual Damages (EAD) in million \pounds\ for individual counties under each climate scenario, illustrating spatial heterogeneity. Red jittered points mark the upper end of the 5th–95th percentile range of 1-in-200 year equivalent damages for counties in selected decades under the High Risk scenario.}
>   \label{fig:dataset-adm3}
> \end{figure}
